      "knowledge_point_id": "92e86e5f-31cd-57ae-a142-023c816d0b33",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Trigonometric Function",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
